sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch06-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Despite differing in detail for almost every rule, the patterns produced by cellular automata of all kinds from random initial conditions can almost always be easily assigned to one of just four classes: uniform final state (class 1), simple stable or periodic structures (class 2), apparently random behavior (class 3), and interacting localized structures (class 4).";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Cellular automaton behavior falls into four basic classes";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 6, “Starting from Randomness”; section “Four Classes of Behavior” (from p. 231), p. 231. Scope: all kinds of cellular automata evolved from random initial conditions"
}
